        {%- assign pd_abs = "Lattice-based cryptography is among the most promising candidates for post-quantum security, and the NIST standardization process has selected Falcon for its compact signatures and fast verification. Its signing procedure, however, relies on floating-point Gaussian sampling over a tree of the secret basis, which makes it hard to implement efficiently and in constant time. Mitaka was proposed as a simpler alternative with a hybrid sampler that avoids the tree and is easier to parallelize. In this work we present the first high-throughput implementations of both schemes on graphics processors. We map the fast Fourier transform, the sampler and the number-theoretic transform onto the thread hierarchy of the GPU, keep the intermediate polynomials in shared memory, and batch thousands of independent signatures so that every streaming multiprocessor is occupied. On an NVIDIA A100 our Falcon implementation signs and verifies tens of thousands of messages per second, and Mitaka is faster still for signing. We compare the two in throughput, latency, memory footprint and numerical behaviour, discuss how their samplers map to the hardware, and identify where each is preferable for servers that must authenticate many clients at once, such as certificate authorities and content-delivery networks." -%}
